  

<h2 id="record-tasks-in-chronological-order-do-them-when-youre-truly-ready">Record tasks in chronological order, do them when you’re truly ready</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/1f500585-aa7a-49bd-b72d-7939b065a93f/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260718153422.png" alt="Photo of an open A6 notebook with handwritten lists and a mechanical pencil in the centre. The book is lying on carpet and there are glimpses of a little girl’s slippers nearby" width="1764" height="1208"></p>
<p>Another week to match the <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/07/13/ptpl-216-plain-text-low-tech-links" class="wikilink">last one</a>: full of caring for little people, my grandbabies. Oh, the noise and delights and the detritus of young family life! My screen time is way down, in the nicest of ways.</p>
<p>The Obsidian queries that run my <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/02/23/filter-todotxt-in-obsidian-no-plugins" class="wikilink">plugin-free todo.txt system</a> are still working, but they aren’t a good fit for this particular season of life.</p>
<p>What is working well is a simple list of tasks split into Home and Work, and run according to <a href="https://archive.constantcontact.com/fs004/1100358239599/archive/1109511856508.html" target="_blank">Mark Forster’s <span class="small-caps">FV</span> time management system</a>. This is a way of tackling a list of tasks according to your psychological readiness to do them.</p>
<p>Take your time reading and re-reading the instructions! (<a href="https://blog.beeminder.com/forster/" target="_blank">Here’s another explanation you might prefer.</a>) It’s not difficult to grasp, but you want to be sure that what you believe you understand about it matches what the system was intended to be.</p>
<p>I appreciate the emphasis on simplicity; tasks are added to the next available line in the list. Something else I love about it is that you can cross off a task when you’ve worked on it, even if it’s not completed. What remains of the task is then re-written at the bottom of the list. This is validating and freeing rather than onerous.</p>
<p>Yet, it’s important not to dive in too deeply too soon.</p>
<p>In Mark’s words —</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The best way to sink any time management system is to overload it right at the beginning. <span class="small-caps">FV</span> is pretty resilient, but at this stage you aren’t. So build up the list gradually.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Forster lists can work with paper or plain text lists. There’s even an <a href="https://bsoule.com/dotlist/" target="_blank">online demo</a> to walk you through the process. For my present situation, a spread in my <span class="small-caps">A6</span> Hobonichi squared notebook is just the right amount of cognitive load.</p>
<p>I’m looking forward to studying Mark Forster’s work in more detail.</p>
